description:
server/mod.rs:376-388 logs every request with the full uri (query string INCLUDED) and client_ip. On a laptop that is debugging output. On a public archive it is a log of who searched for what, retained for as long as the container logs are.

For a tool aimed at libraries and archives this deserves an explicit position rather than a default inherited from development convenience. Reader privacy is a professional norm in that world, and 'we keep every search query against an IP' is a sentence an operator may need to answer for.

Options, cheapest first:
- drop or truncate the query string in the span, keeping the path (most of the debugging value is in the route, not the terms);
- hash or truncate the IP (/24, /48) so rate-limit forensics still work without identifying a reader;
- make the whole thing configurable, defaulting to the private choice;
- document it either way in the deploy guide, including that Caddy keeps its OWN access log with the same data, so a decision in indice alone does not cover the deployment.

NOTE the tension with .1: per-IP rate limiting needs to know the IP, at least in memory. Logging it is a separate decision from using it, and the distinction is the point of this ticket.

Also worth a line in the docs: <home>/events/ and index/waczs.json already hold SubjectIds derived from login addresses (DESIGN says so, and reference/home-directory.md:81 warns about committing events/). That is about CONTRIBUTORS; this ticket is about READERS. Both belong in whatever privacy section the deploy guide grows.
